@@ -69,6 +69,31 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as text with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is the JSON re-serialized over multiple lines, indented by 4 spaces by default, and highlighted with the ``json.*`` styles from the console theme. Here's an example::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The ``indent`` argument sets the number of spaces to indent, and ``highlight`` disables highlighting when set to ``False``. Invalid JSON raises the parser's error and prints nothing.
+
+You can also log a JSON document by passing a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, so its lines are never wrapped. Default printing still crops lines wider than the console width; pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to keep full lines.
+
+The package has a :func:`rich.print_json` function which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "Rich"}')
+
+Rich can also pretty print a JSON file from the command line::
+
+    $ python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
